      <workList xml:id="workDesc_fc7be34e">
         <work xml:id="work_d1e224221">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_dcbeb7b3">B5:Coll.005</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_f0043df1" xml:lang="de">Preussische Kriegslieder</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_fd0e4b9e">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_edb1e7fa">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_7eafebf6">Johann Wilhelm Ludwig Gleim</persName>
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_443d0257">Friedrich Gottlieb Klopstock</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_fde860e9">
               <date notafter="1761" notbefore="1756" xml:id="date_afeefabd">c. 1760</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_fcaf0a37">
               <p xml:id="idm87">When the Seven Years' War broke out in 1756, Scheibe was living in the southern part of Denmark keeping a correspondence with musicians such as <persName xml:id="idm88" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Joachim Quantz</persName> and <persName xml:id="idm89" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Carl Heinrich Graun</persName>, both employed at the court in Berlin. The tense political climate and the tension of conflict in Germany and its possible negative outcome for Danish political and diplomatic relations with Prussia might have drawn Scheibe to seek closer ties with the court of <persName xml:id="idm90" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Frederick the Great</persName>. Thus he composed, for instance, a cantata for the king’s birthday (SchW B2:434). The present eight songs for voice and figured bass may be dated to the late 1750s and beginning of the 1760s. Seven of the poems were written by <persName xml:id="idm91" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Johann Wilhelm Ludwig Gleim</persName> and published in <rend xml:id="idm92" fontstyle="italic">Preussische Kriegslieder in den Feldzügen 1756 und 1757</rend> (Berlin, 1758), praising the triumphs of Frederick the Great. The edition also included settings of some of the poems by the composer <persName xml:id="idm93" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Christian Gottfried Krause</persName> and Scheibe who knew Krause (the reviewer of Scheibe's opera 'Thusnelde'; SchW B1:002) chose some of the same poems. However, two of the poems which Scheibe used do not appear in that edition: ‘Kommt, Brüder! singt zum neuen Jahr’ (SchW B5:176), also by Gleim, was published in 1760 following the recapture of Breslau; the second poem, ‘Die Schlacht geht an’ (SchW B5:177), is not by Gleim but by one of Gleim's friends, <persName xml:id="idm94" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Friedrich Gottlieb Klopstock</persName>. It was finished already in 1749 though it remained in manuscript until 1771. In the published version the first line has been changed to 'Der Feind ist da! die Schlacht beginnt!'. Scheibe might have received a manuscript copy from Klopstock, who mainly lived in Copenhagen from 1751 until 1770. Though Scheibe was often in Copenhagen participating in various events at The Musical Society during their concert seasons as well as supplying music for the court, he and his family finally returned to Copenhagen in 1761, around which time he would have had ample opportunity to discuss issues such as text, music and rhythm with Klopstock. The Seven Years’ War ended in 1763. Scheibe’s songs were never published remaining in manuscript throughout his life. It is difficult to ascertain which text sources Scheibe might have employed. Firstly, he only gives the first couple of words from the first line and, secondly, many different editions were published throughout the period, very often as pamphlets. The most likely sources are therefore listed in the bibliography rather than under 'Sources'.</p>
            </history>
